# Turn records for picture and clip conversations. A conversation with any picture or clip got no record, so after a
# restart, or from a client that drops the thinking, it could not resume; and a screenshot tool result (a tool message
# whose content is a part list) never got a verified start, so in a screenshot loop no turn got a label at all. Now a
# picture request is seen through one marked render as production renders it (the media split out, a mark before each
# picture and clip, quoted markers escaped as production escapes them; a tool result sent as a plain part list is
# marked too) and must equal production's text; each picture's identity (9013) is hashed into the label at its place
# (an M record after its span's text), so the same words with another picture never share a label (Mia's reasoning
# memory gave such a conversation another's thinking). The record (r5) holds T keyed (picture rows hold -1 - their
# key), its sha256 over int64, and a picture table (kind, rows, key); its key also carries the label without the
# pictures, which only names a miss ("pictures differ"). On a hit the layout is rebuilt: the record's tokens plus the
# new part laid out with the request's own new pictures and clips, after checking that the record's pictures are the
# request's first ones (rows and keys), that T holds each at its rows, and that the new part's tokens are production's
# own tail; the prompt and the layout go to the engine together. The engine compares keyed tails when a request
# inherits its state's record. A reply that writes a picture or clip marker keeps no record. Measured on the CPU with the
# real template, tokenizer and picture frontend: every turn of an 11-turn 1080p screenshot loop past its 8th picture,
# picture and clip conversations in memory and after a restart, and the Anthropic and Responses routes are served their
# records, token for token what a fresh read of the true conversation prefills. mgllm, 2026-10-08.
# Tests: tests/test_9015_picture_records.py (CPU; needs TF_GLM_TEST_MODEL_DIR, torchvision and PyAV).

